    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "We conducted a suite of experiments in which we produced tensile microcracks during dynamic rupture along an interface with frictional and cohesive strength. Ruptures were induced in Homalite samples by exploding a nickel-cadmium wire embedded along the interface. During the experiments, the samples were held under a vertical axial load, P, and rupture was induced along an interface inclined and an angle 􏰃 to the horizontal. Test values of 􏰃 were 60o, and 70o and 80o, and P varied between ~85% and 98% of the static load under which the samples failed in preliminary strength tests (e.g., 34-38 MPa for the experiments with 􏰃􏰄=70) . The dynamic stress fields produced by the exploding wire are recorded using photoelasticity and high-speed digital photography.\r\n\r\nRupture velocities during the experiments ranged between 70% and 85% of the shear wave speed of Homalite (cs ≈ 1200 m/s). Tensile microcracks were produced on the tensile side of the rupture interface in the Homalite samples during rupture with a variety of different combinations of 􏰃 and P. We associate microcrack formation with the transient stress perturbation due to the dynamic because symmetric caustics (shadow spots) can be seen at the tips of growing micrcracks trailing the main shear rupture tip in the high speed photoelastic images. We use measurements of microcrack orientation to constrain the velocity weakening behavior of the rupture tip, characterized by a velocity weakening parameter 􏰅. Using this parameter, we show that microcrack orientation is a function of the rupture velocity and the global static stress field. Ultimately, the results of this study will be combined with observations of secondary tensile fractures along exposed faults to create diagnostic criteria for interpreting the velocity and directivity of past ruptures.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
